# Licence for the data

This dataset holds three kinds of material, and they are not under the same
terms: the code, what this project added to the Senate's record, and the
Senate's own words and documents.

## The code — MIT

Everything under `scripts/` (and, in the repository, `notebooks/`), and the
prose of the documentation, is MIT-licensed. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).

## What this project added — CC BY 4.0

The work of turning the record into a dataset is licensed **Creative Commons
Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)**:
<https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/>

That covers:

- in `data/processed/senado/`, the structure of the corpus and everything
  said about each passage: how the record is divided into passages, their
  types and note types, the speaker labels as fields, turns, sections, page
  positions and typography, the resolved speakers and their caucuses
  (`speakers.parquet`), and the parse record (`parse_stats.csv`). It does not
  cover the words in the `text` column; see below.
- `reference/senado/`: the tables this project compiled — the caucus
  observations and the caucus lives dated by hand, the authorities observed
  on the cover pages, the mappings of sitting labels. `senadores_historico.json`
  is excluded: it is a copy of the Senate's open data.
- `reference/gold/` and `reference/gold_html/`: the annotations (the `.json`
  files, the stretch list and the briefs). The page images in
  `reference/gold/pages/` and the fragments of the chamber's HTML in
  `reference/gold_html/stretches/` are the Senate's; see below.
- `raw_data_manifest.csv`: the provenance manifest.
- `reference/verification/`, in the repository only: the blind-read
  records, which are not part of a release.

You may copy, redistribute and build on this material, including
commercially, provided you give attribution. The citation to use is in
[README.md](README.md), and in machine-readable form in `CITATION.cff`.

## The Senate's words and documents — not relicensed

The speeches, the stenographers' notes and every other word of the record are
the Argentine Senate's and its members', not this project's. This project
grants no licence to them. That covers:

- the `text` column of the per-sitting tables, which reproduces the record;
- the page images in `reference/gold/pages/` and the HTML fragments in
  `reference/gold_html/stretches/`, copied from the chamber's files so the
  annotations can be checked against what they annotate;
- `reference/senado/senadores_historico.json`, the Senate's list of former
  and serving senators as its open-data portal serves it;
- the archived pages under `data/raw/senado/bloques_archivados/` and
  `data/raw/senado/fichas_archivadas/`: Internet Archive captures of Senate
  pages that no longer exist, the chamber's caucus roster and senators' own
  pages, kept because the caucuses before 2005 cannot be rebuilt without them.
  Each row derived from them carries the archive URL it came from.

The source transcripts themselves are not redistributed: the manifest
identifies each file by checksum and URL, and `scripts/download.py` fetches it
again.

Two points of Argentine law bear on reusing this material. Neither is a
grant from the chamber or from this project, and neither is legal advice.
Argentina's access-to-information law (Ley 27.275) obliges the state to
publish its information in formats that allow reuse. Copyright law (Ley
11.723, art. 27) allows parliamentary speeches to be published, but not for
profit without the speaker's authorisation. Commercial use of the text is
therefore a matter between the user and the rights holders.
